WEBVTT

s1.1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.479
Seiji lends iOS simulators on a shared Mac to agents, over MCP.

s1.2
00:00:05.479 --> 00:00:09.018
Each lease gets a fresh simulator. Seiji deletes it at release.

s1.3
00:00:09.018 --> 00:00:16.520
Seiji records each run: a video, the app log, a screenshot of each step, and a manifest with SHA-256s.

s1.4
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.844
A ticket for a mobile app needs that proof.

s1.5
00:00:18.844 --> 00:00:23.273
The agent runs the app on a real simulator. The reviewers see what happened.

s2.1
00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:27.171
An admin adds Seiji with one command and the seiji preset.

s2.2
00:00:27.171 --> 00:00:32.904
The token goes in on standard input, as an Authorization header. Symphony never shows it again.

s2.3
00:00:32.904 --> 00:00:40.213
The preset fills the HTTP API root, a 65-minute timeout, two agent variables, and a cleanup rule.

s2.4
00:00:40.213 --> 00:00:44.603
Symphony stores a plain definition. Explicit flags win over the preset.

s2.5
00:00:44.603 --> 00:00:47.773
Tern's Admin, MCP screen has the same preset.

s2.6
00:00:47.773 --> 00:00:53.275
Test lists Seiji's 23 tools and the protocol, 2025-11-25.

s2.7
00:00:53.275 --> 00:00:57.113
It also reads Seiji's /v1/me, and shows the user and the quota.

s3.1
00:00:57.463 --> 00:00:59.946
Symphony keeps the Seiji token in a Secret.

s3.2
00:00:59.946 --> 00:01:04.504
The agent gets SEIJI_TOKEN. That is its own session token, not Seiji's.

s3.3
00:01:04.504 --> 00:01:07.579
SEIJI_URL points at the gateway, not at Seiji.

s3.4
00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:11.918
The gateway checks the session token. Then it sends the stored token to Seiji.

s3.5
00:01:11.918 --> 00:01:18.571
It does this for MCP calls and for the HTTP API: uploads, evidence files, and /v1/me.

s3.6
00:01:18.571 --> 00:01:22.366
It rewrites Seiji's links to the gateway, so the agent can follow them.

s3.7
00:01:22.366 --> 00:01:26.407
Viewer pages take no token, so the gateway does not proxy them.

s4.1
00:01:26.757 --> 00:01:30.824
The ticket ENG-163 is a crash on the sign-in screen.

s4.2
00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:34.179
The agent fixes it, then acquires an iPhone simulator.

s4.3
00:01:34.179 --> 00:01:38.735
It uploads the build through SEIJI_URL. The upload streams through the gateway.

s4.4
00:01:38.735 --> 00:01:42.644
It runs the sign-in flow with run_actions. Seiji records the run.

s4.5
00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:47.905
export_evidence returns a Markdown report. The agent puts it in the pull request body.

s4.6
00:01:47.905 --> 00:01:52.566
Reviewers cannot open Seiji's links, so the agent attaches the files to the ticket.

s4.7
00:01:52.566 --> 00:01:57.276
Linear takes evidence.zip. A GitHub issue takes report.md.

s5.1
00:01:57.626 --> 00:01:59.655
A lease costs budget until it ends.

s5.2
00:01:59.655 --> 00:02:03.181
The gateway records each lease that acquire_device returns.

s5.3
00:02:03.181 --> 00:02:06.862
When the attempt ends, Symphony releases every lease that is still held.

s5.4
00:02:06.862 --> 00:02:12.225
If the runner crashes, a sweep every 60 seconds releases the leases of expired sessions.

s5.5
00:02:12.225 --> 00:02:15.183
It keeps trying for 24 hours, then it gives up.

s5.6
00:02:15.183 --> 00:02:20.236
Without this, a device waits for Seiji's idle reaper, 15 minutes by default.

s6.1
00:02:20.586 --> 00:02:23.397
A session that holds a device shows a Device row.

s6.2
00:02:23.397 --> 00:02:26.040
Press W to watch the device, view only.

s6.3
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.003
Shift W gives you control, after a confirmation.

s6.4
00:02:29.003 --> 00:02:32.736
Your taps reach the device at once, and other people may be watching.

s6.5
00:02:32.736 --> 00:02:38.940
Each press gets a fresh link from symphony mcp viewer. The link opens once, within 60 seconds.

s6.6
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.835
Only a lease that a live session holds can be watched.

s6.7
00:02:41.835 --> 00:02:47.462
Seiji's own Tern plugin can run next to Symphony's. They share no id, key or command.

s7.1
00:02:47.812 --> 00:02:51.795
When CI uploads a preview build of a pull request, the merger finds it.

s7.2
00:02:51.795 --> 00:02:57.189
It posts Open in simulator in Slack. A reviewer clicks it, and gets a viewer link privately.

s7.3
00:02:57.189 --> 00:03:01.837
The merger also keeps one Preview comment on the pull request, with a link per build.

s7.4
00:03:01.837 --> 00:03:06.501
A link has no expiry. It opens that build while GitHub keeps the artifact.

s7.5
00:03:06.501 --> 00:03:12.756
Behind Cloudflare Access, any mapped person may open it. Without Access, only signed public links work.

s7.6
00:03:12.756 --> 00:03:15.960
The page shows the progress, then a Seiji control viewer.

s7.7
00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.390
Each build gets its own device, at most two per pull request.

s7.8
00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:24.514
A device goes back after 55 minutes, or when the pull request is merged or closed.

s7.9
00:03:24.514 --> 00:03:27.875
If Seiji ended the lease first, the link starts the build again.

s7.10
00:03:27.875 --> 00:03:33.103
In chat, share_device posts a Control the device button. Only the asker gets the link.

s8.1
00:03:33.453 --> 00:03:37.955
Mocked scenario 13 passed every check in 55.9 seconds.

s8.2
00:03:37.955 --> 00:03:42.128
The cleanup released the one held lease 0.02 seconds after the attempt.

s8.3
00:03:42.128 --> 00:03:46.029
The mock saw only the stored token. The session token never reached it.

s8.4
00:03:46.029 --> 00:03:51.731
A test with the real Seiji ran through the gateway. Real omp listed the 23 tools.

s8.5
00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:56.702
Real Seiji refused the session token with 401, and accepted the stored token.

s8.6
00:03:56.702 --> 00:04:01.129
No real device was booted yet. That needs a Mac with Xcode simulators.

s8.7
00:04:01.129 --> 00:04:05.509
All agents share one Seiji user, with its budget and its concurrency limit.

s8.8
00:04:05.509 --> 00:04:10.344
On Kubernetes, Seiji needs a public TLS name in listen.allowedHosts.

s8.9
00:04:10.344 --> 00:04:13.176
The Seiji guide in the docs has every step.
